bout time that  every  young african wherever they live had  access to  the internet had access to electricity  uh  and that could only happen if we embrace  the idea that we can  decentralize electricity generation  bring it to  villages everywhere and for that you  need renewable energy for that you need  solar power you need wind  you will need hydrogen at some point and  also in all these  things uh we can work together and and  specifically on south africa and  a number of other countries you need to  wean yourselves of coal which is going  to be a  huge huge challenge as well  absolutely um i mean how do you respond  to the argument that  well you know you know europe um was  able to benefit from  from very cheap fossil fuels for decades  to fuel  its growth and africa should be allowed  to do the same thing  i think africa has a huge opportunity  with its young and vibrant  population to leapfrog over a number of  developments and to at least  avoid some of the worst mistakes we made  in europe  uh over the last 150 years um  so if you first create a bigger  dependency  on fossil fuels to then have to wean  yourselves from that  is more difficult than to immediately  try and embrace  uh new forms of energy and i'm more and  more enthusiastic about  uh what i see in in in different in  western africa in some countries in  eastern africa  as in ethiopia earlier this year and  also there you know the understanding  that you can have  villages with their own power generation  based on solar and wind  is bringing sort of it's it's also a  form of emancipation and it's also a  form of  bringing more social justice also to  remoter areas and countries  mr timmermans you have been leading  efforts to introduce  a green deal in europe um  can you tell us a little bit about what  this might mean for africa  i think it would mean that you  uh bring new forms of energy generation  or or  upscale them the potential for solar  energy in africa is huge  don't have to explain that the potential  for carbon sink you know through your  natural  environment through forests and  grasslands etc is also huge  um potential to because you know  we also have a biodiversity crisis we're  losing  one million species if we're not very  careful the potential for africa  to contribute to our biodiversity  resilience worldwide is huge we need to  stop  uh a deforestation of primal forests in  africa that's  of huge importance but especially the  potential for younger people to learn  trades to  study to be part of an economy that's  going to be extremely  uh vibrant is huge you know africa has  such potential  and the green deal is a growth strategy  it's not just about  tackling the climate crisis it's also  about bringing about  new forms of economy a circular economy  no longer based on fossil fuels no  longer based on  on wasting commodities but rather on  recycling and on reusing etc  and for africa this has a huge uh  potential you know recycling and reusing  is not something  most africans are unfamiliar with how  you know when you have these  conversations with other african leaders  um  how receptive are they to these kinds of  ideas  well i think that the  what's driving this i think frankly is  worries about climate change which i see  have increased  uh exponentially in in most if not all  african countries  secondly they see economic opportunity  um given the fact that we're also in an  industrial revolution and as i said  before  africa would have the possibility to  leapfrog over a number of stages in our  economic development if it is done right  thirdly i think what really is important  to note  is that more and more european countries  are  not talking to african countries by  saying we know what's good for you  but saying we're listening to you how  can we help  how can we be part of this because we  see opportunities  for us as well and then of course  there's also the elements  that i really welcome in an increasing  number of african countries  the the societal structures we have in  europe uh the rule of law freedom of the  media  uh a vibrant ngo community things that  um  especially many young africans aspire to  as well and and if politicians have any  sense they they would cater to those  aspirations rather than suppress them  there was a major vote in the european  parliament  to cut carbon emissions by 60 percent by  the year 2030.  first of all congratulations that's a  major accomplishment  but what we have seen already is that  you know  when confronting carbon emissions some  companies  choose to simply outsource those  emissions to other parts of the world  um how are you going to prevent european  companies and countries from simply  outsourcing their emissions to africa  yeah this is i'm really glad you raised  this because  this is a trap we should avoid we've  seen it before with waste  with plastics you know we get rid of our  problem by  bringing that stuff elsewhere and we  should avoid this from happening here  this will depend a lot  on the strategic partnerships we want to  develop with african countries  it would also depend a lot on the  behavior of private investors that we  can influence  so how can africa contribute to this i  think  to look very much at governance uh  investment protection at  working together with us to make sure  that we can attract  investment and also we should you know  we should  address the issue of of global corporate  governance  uh with a lot of force you know if you  if europeans get all worked up  over deforestation in africa in latin  america  they should all get worked up as well  about who's responsible for this  uh and and sometimes the responsibility  lies with the european companies so  we also have an issue to tackle here of  corporate  responsibility and i i hope we can  convince the majority in the european  parliament and in the council  uh to hold our companies accountable for  this  and if they don't uh if they're not  accountable that would have consequences  for their cooperation and their support  in this transition so just to clarify  will you be pushing for  legislation that that specifically um  regulates the way that companies behave  in other parts of the world  well first of all i think this should be  part and parcel of any trade  agreements we sign with other parts of  the world  secondly i think this should be part of  the uh  strategic agreements we reach with the  african union and with  individual african nations thirdly i  think  uh corporate uh governance uh  internationally  should also be part of the proposals we  make  for legislation in europe and i know my  colleagues are working on this we've  been working on this in the past but we  need to  improve that um so i think on all these  levels we can  make sure that we don't have these  negative consequences that are indeed  a trap we should avoid i want to get on  to the issue of  migration of course we know that there  has been a wave of migration from  african countries to europe  driven at least in part by changing  climate conditions  we've seen the conditions in camps like  moria in greece we've seen the boats  left to drown in the mediterranean at  the same time  you know you've had instances of europe  giving money to dictators like omar  al-bashir to try and prevent migration  from occurring  in this context you know how can we  trust europe to do the right thing  well i think the only way we could  actually  come to solutions that are effective  but also morally acceptable is  when we address the root causes of all  this you know once  young people have paid smugglers  in their home countries and are on their  way  and and risk their lives in the sahara  or elsewhere and then mr lives again on  sea  it's in fact already too late you know  the only way we're going to solve this  is if  young people have enough confidence in  their own futures in their own countries  that they don't feel the need to migrate  or when they feel the need to migrate to  work in europe that we have a legal  system whereby they can apply for visas  in a way that is realistic  in a way that gives them an opportunity  to come to europe and i think  only if we do that can we find solutions  i mean  i hear what you're saying but you know  we've got countries like like italy and  greece which have taken a very hard-line  approach to  both refugees asylum seekers  and economic migrants you know arriving  on their shores  how are you going to get them to toe the  line well first of all  we need to be fair to those countries  for many many many years they've had  a very liberal very open attitude to  refugees  and then of course uh because of the war  in syria and because of other reasons  they were at some point overwhelmed they  asked their european friends  to help them and got very little  almost no response and at some point  they started to wave through  um these migrants and refugees  and then other other countries in europe  uh accused that  them of not following the rules which  they didn't but they had  asked for help for such a long time so  the problem here is in essence a problem  of moral hazard within the european  union  european nations who don't trust each  other some nations who think they are  doing too much  other nations are saying yeah i'm not at  the border but  all these refugees end up in my country  although i'm not at the border  and also a third group of nations uh who  say we can't be bothered  you're on your own we're not helping and  all of this has contributed to  to to a very poisonous cocktail of moral  hazard  in europe and and and the ones who pay  the highest price in this context  context are the refugees and the  migrants so that's why we're trying to  to change that paradigm to to to come up  with ideas  realistic ideas about shared solidarity  about  sharing the burden uh but also uh  realistic solutions to provide clarity  in the shortest possible time to people  who present themselves at european  borders demanding asylum  whether they have the right to that  asylum or not  what are some of those those solutions  as i said  um you know if we if we don't talk about  tackling the issue  at the source we'll not succeed so we  need to provide we need to do everything  we can  to provide more opportunity in african  countries themselves  sometimes countries grow very quickly  but then the economic growth  falls into the hands of a very very  small elite and even very well-educated  young people feel  cut out of their own society so they  look um  for opportunity elsewhere and and this  has to change i mean  i i think there's probably very few  african leaders who don't understand  that they need to change this so  where we can contribute through an  erasmus program which which offers  higher education  uh through training on the spot through  other means through investment as i said  before  to create a better opportunity i think  this is what we should should be doing  but once people um do come to europe  we need to to have procedures in place  and that's what what the commissioners  proposed a couple of weeks ago  that would offer especially from those  countries  people come from where we know they will  never have  the right to asylum because they don't  flee from war or natural disasters  that they get clarity very quickly  because now they can just play the  system very often  sometimes even for years coming from  countries where we  absolutely know they have nothing to  fear and still they can you know  if it gives them two years in europe  that they can work illegally etc  for two years those are for young people  these at least two years they're not  starving or they're not  they're not in in dire straits and even  if at the end they're then sent back  sometimes you know we're i think only  one third of the people are  actually sent back uh who don't have the  right to asylum so  that's part of the system that